<% content_for :title, "An appeal to Bible publishers" %>

<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 py-12">
  <header class="border-b border-[var(--color-rule)] pb-6 mb-12">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">An open letter</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1 leading-tight">
      An appeal to Bible publishers: free your texts
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 italic text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] font-serif">
      To the publishers whose editions shape how the world reads scripture.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="font-serif text-[var(--color-ink)] leading-relaxed text-lg space-y-5
                  [&_h2]:font-serif [&_h2]:text-2xl [&_h2]:text-[var(--color-ink)] [&_h2]:mt-12 [&_h2]:mb-3
                  [&_h2]:tracking-tight
                  [&_p]:max-w-prose
                  [&_strong]:font-semibold">

    <p>
      The texts you publish are humanity&rsquo;s common inheritance. The
      manuscripts are public domain by virtue of age. The languages are
      public domain. The methods of textual criticism that produced your
      editions are public domain. What is not public domain is your
      editorial work on top of them: the translations, the critical
      apparatus, the text-critical decisions, the verse divisions,
      the cross-references, the formatting.
    </p>

    <p>
      We write to ask you to release that editorial work under a free
      license.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "The scriptural argument", id: "scriptural" %>

    <figure class="my-6 pl-6 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-rule)] not-italic">
      <blockquote class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
        <p>Assemble the people, the men and the women and the little
        ones, and thy stranger that is within thy gates, that they may
        hear, and that they may learn&hellip;</p>
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption class="text-sm italic mt-1 text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
        &mdash; Deuteronomy 31:12 (JPS 1917)
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Every major religious tradition that uses these texts teaches that
      scripture should be accessible to all. Locking the best available
      edition behind a licensing wall is a choice that needs to be
      justified against that tradition, not the other way around.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "The argument from the law", id: "the-law" %>

    <p>
      The right to enforce copyright is a legal right. It means the right
      to sue &mdash; to bring a brother or sister before a secular court and
      compel them, under threat of penalty, to stop sharing the words of
      the prophets and apostles. That is what copyright enforcement is.
      The question is whether this is consistent with what the New
      Testament itself teaches about how believers should use the law.
    </p>

    <figure class="my-6 pl-6 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-rule)] not-italic">
      <blockquote class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
        <p>Dare any of you, having a matter against another, go to law
        before the unjust, and not before the saints?&hellip; Now
        therefore there is utterly a fault among you, because ye go to
        law one with another. Why do ye not rather take wrong? why do ye
        not rather suffer yourselves to be defrauded?</p>
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption class="text-sm italic mt-1 text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
        &mdash; 1 Corinthians 6:1, 7 (KJV)
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Paul&rsquo;s instruction is not ambiguous. He does not say
      &ldquo;go to law only when you have a good reason.&rdquo; He says
      it is <em>utterly a fault</em> &mdash; a defeat, a moral failure
      &mdash; to go to law against a fellow believer at all, and that it
      would be better to be defrauded than to seek legal remedy. This is
      not a marginal teaching. It is a direct apostolic instruction about
      how the community of believers should resolve disputes.
    </p>

    <figure class="my-6 pl-6 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-rule)] not-italic">
      <blockquote class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
        <p>But I say unto you, That ye resist not evil: but whosoever
        shall smite thee on thy right cheek, turn to him the other
        also. And if any man will sue thee at the law, and take away thy
        coat, let him have thy cloak also.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption class="text-sm italic mt-1 text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
        &mdash; Matthew 5:39&ndash;40 (KJV)
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Christ himself, in the Sermon on the Mount, taught that when someone
      takes legal action against you, the right response is not to
      counter-sue but to give more than was demanded. The entire thrust of
      his teaching about the law is that love supersedes legal right, and
      that holding onto what is legally yours at the cost of relationship
      is a loss, not a victory.
    </p>

    <p>
      A publisher who holds copyright on a Bible translation and enforces
      it against other Christians is exercising a legal right that Christ
      and Paul explicitly taught should be surrendered. If a church prints
      your translation in its bulletin without permission, Paul says you
      should suffer the loss. If a developer includes your text in a free
      Bible app, Christ says give them the study notes too.
    </p>

    <p>
      If publishers believe these teachings, the credible signal is to
      act on them: release the text under a license that makes enforcement
      unnecessary, because the right has been given up. A free license is
      not generosity &mdash; it is obedience.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "A note of understanding", id: "understanding" %>

    <p>
      We recognise that this is a radical reading. Most publishers and
      most Christians do not interpret Paul&rsquo;s instruction as
      applying to intellectual property, and many will find it
      unreasonable to ask a publisher to surrender the legal protection
      that funds their editorial work.
    </p>

    <p>
      We understand. We do not judge publishers who choose to retain
      copyright. The economic pressures are real. The editorial work is
      real. The desire to protect a text from misuse is real. A publisher
      who has spent decades on a translation has earned the right to
      decide how it is distributed, and we respect that decision even
      when we disagree with it.
    </p>

    <p>
      But understanding is not the same as participation. Open
      Scriptorium cannot host texts whose license depends on the ongoing
      goodwill of a rights holder &mdash; not because we doubt the
      goodwill, but because a text that can be withdrawn is not truly
      free. Our readers and contributors need the certainty that every
      text in the corpus will remain available to them, to their
      children, and to anyone who forks this project a hundred years
      from now. That certainty is what a free license provides, and it
      is what &ldquo;used by permission&rdquo; does not.
    </p>

    <p>
      The door is open. If your position changes, the corpus is ready.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "The economic argument", id: "economic" %>

    <p>
      The market for print Bibles is not threatened by free digital text.
      People buy physical Bibles for the weight that says <em>this
      matters</em>, for ink that sits on the page with a presence no
      screen can match, for the binding, the study aids, the trust
      a publisher has earned.
    </p>

    <p>
      Scripture is not a product that loses value with exposure. It is
      the kind of text that, the more a person reads, the more they
      want to read. Free digital availability does not cannibalize
      print sales; it puts the most compelling material in human
      history into more hands, and those hands reach for study
      editions, reference Bibles, and leather-bound gift copies.
      Every freely-available digital Bible is an on-ramp to the
      publisher&rsquo;s print ecosystem.
    </p>

    <p>
      The most successful Bible in the English language has been free for
      over three centuries. The King James Version is public domain, and
      publishers continue to make millions selling it in print. Free
      access did not destroy the market; it created it. The KJV proves
      the model: a free text sustains an entire industry of study editions,
      devotional formats, leather bindings, and gift Bibles. Among modern
      translations, the Berean Standard Bible has been released into the
      public domain. Its interlinear data is the richest digital Bible
      dataset in existence, and bereanbible.com is thriving.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "The legacy argument", id: "legacy" %>

    <p>
      Tischendorf released his edition into the public domain. Westcott and
      Hort released theirs. Nestle 1904 is public domain. These are the
      editions scholars still cite 150 years later. The editions locked
      behind copyright are the ones that get replaced and forgotten.
    </p>

    <p>
      A publisher who releases their apparatus under CC BY ensures their
      editorial decisions become the permanent reference. A publisher who
      locks it behind a paywall ensures someone will eventually redo the
      work and render theirs obsolete.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "The practical argument", id: "practical" %>

    <p>
      Restrictive licensing fragments the ecosystem. Five different apps
      each license the same text under five different terms, each with
      slightly different restrictions. Scholars cannot build interoperable
      tools. To read two translations of the same psalm side by side, you
      need permission from two different rights holders. The words of the
      prophets are subject to a terms-of-service agreement.
    </p>

    <p>
      Free licensing unifies the ecosystem. One canonical digital text,
      cited everywhere, improved by everyone. The publisher&rsquo;s name
      stays on it forever.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "The case against non-commercial clauses", id: "against-nc" %>

    <p>
      Some publishers offer their texts under licences that permit free
      use but prohibit commercial use. This sounds like a reasonable
      compromise. It is not.
    </p>

    <p>
      The term &ldquo;commercial&rdquo; has no settled legal definition
      in this context. Is a church that sells a study guide containing
      the text engaged in commerce? Is a nonprofit Bible app with a
      donation button? A seminary that charges tuition? A podcast that
      reads scripture on an episode with sponsors? No court has drawn a
      clear line, and the Creative Commons organisation itself has
      acknowledged the ambiguity. The result is that everyone who
      touches the text must make their own guess about what is permitted
      &mdash; and most guess conservatively, which means doing nothing.
    </p>

    <p>
      A non-commercial clause does not protect revenue. It protects
      uncertainty. The publisher gains nothing from a seminary deciding
      not to use their text out of caution. They gain nothing from a
      developer choosing a different translation because the legal risk
      of NC is not worth evaluating. The practical effect of NC is not
      to prevent exploitation &mdash; it is to prevent adoption.
    </p>

    <p>
      CC BY accomplishes everything NC claims to accomplish, without the
      ambiguity. Attribution is enforceable. The publisher&rsquo;s name
      stays on the text. Anyone who builds on it must say where it came
      from. What they cannot do is stop someone from using it &mdash;
      which is the point.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "To nonprofits and denominational bodies", id: "nonprofits" %>

    <p>
      Some of the most important texts are controlled not by commercial
      publishers but by nonprofit organisations and denominational bodies.
      The economic arguments above may not apply to you in the same way
      &mdash; your concern is stewardship, not revenue.
    </p>

    <p>
      We understand the instinct to protect a text from misuse. But a
      restrictive licence does not prevent misuse &mdash; it prevents use.
      Anyone determined to distort scripture will do so regardless of your
      licence terms. What the restriction actually prevents is the scholar
      who wants to build a concordance, the developer who wants to make the
      text accessible to the blind, the translator who wants to produce a
      minority-language edition, and the student who wants to compare your
      apparatus with another.
    </p>

    <p>
      CC BY protects against misattribution. It does not protect against
      disagreement. No licence can. The choice is between a text that is
      used &mdash; sometimes in ways you would not choose &mdash; and a
      text that is not used at all.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "The competitive argument", id: "competitive" %>

    <p>
      The Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft&rsquo;s copyright on NA28/UBS5 is the
      single biggest obstacle to digital biblical scholarship. Every year
      that apparatus stays locked, more scholars build tools against free
      alternatives: Robinson&ndash;Pierpont and Nestle 1904. The Center
      for New Testament Restoration has gone
      further, applying statistical methods to 194 digitized manuscript
      transcriptions to produce a free critical text with full apparatus
      under CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0. The lock-in strategy is not just failing
      in slow motion; it is being actively outflanked by open data and
      computational methods that did not exist when the licensing model
      was designed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Publishers who release first get cited first. The first free critical
      apparatus for the Greek NT will become the default reference for a
      generation of scholars. That is a legacy worth more than any
      licensing fee.
    </p>

    <%= anchor_heading "What we are asking", id: "what-we-ask" %>

    <p>
      We are asking publishers to release their editorial work under a
      free license: the text, the apparatus, the study notes, the
      cross-references. The typesetting, layout, and physical product
      remain theirs. A free digital text is not a replacement for a
      printed study Bible &mdash; it is the foundation on which the next
      generation of readers discovers that they need one.
    </p>

    <p>
      CC BY is enough. We do not need CC0. Attribution is fine. We just
      need the four freedoms: to use, study, modify, and redistribute.
    </p>

    <p>
      The texts you publish were given freely. The traditions they
      belong to were transmitted freely. The only thing standing
      between that inheritance and the next generation of readers is
      a licensing decision.
    </p>

    <hr class="border-[var(--color-rule)] my-12">

    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] italic">
      This letter is also addressed to the scholarly societies and
      academic presses that publish reference editions. Please also read
      our
      <%= link_to "appeal to scholars", appeal_to_scholars_path,
            class: "underline decoration-dotted hover:text-[var(--color-link)]" %>,
      which asks editors and lexicographers to release their editorial
      work under free licenses.
    </p>
  </article>
</div>
